  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "The following table shows the values of a continuous function $f(x)$ over the interval $[4,9].$\n\n| $x$ | $4$ | $5.5$ | $8$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $4$ | $6$ | $9.5$ |\n\nUse the right Riemann sum, with the three subintervals indicated by the data, to approximate the area under the curve $y=f(x)$ over the interval $[4,9].$",
  "worked_solution": "In this case, we have $n=3$ rectangles. Using the right Riemann sum, the area is approximated as \n\n$$\nA\u2248f(5.5)\u0394{x}_{1}+f(8)\u0394{x}_{2}+f(9)\u0394{x}_{3},\n$$\n\nwhere each $\u0394{x}_{i}$ is calculated by considering the step between a particular $x$-value and the previous one, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\u0394{x}_{i}={x}_{i}-{x}_{i-1}.\n$$\n\nWe set up a value table that includes the $\u0394x$'s.\n\n| $x$ | $4$ | $5.5$ | $8$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\u0394x$ | $-$ | $1.5$ | $2.5$ | $1$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $-$ | $4$ | $6$ | $9.5$ |\n\nFinally, we plug in the numbers to approximate the area under the curve and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & \u22484\\cdot 1.5+6\\cdot 2.5+9.5\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =6+15+9.5 \\\\  & =30.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
